<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#SacredLanguage> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A language, language register, or language variety used as a liturgical or ritual medium in Afro-Atlantic or related African sacred practice. Includes fully distinct languages (Yorùbá in Lucumí/Candomblé; Kikongo in Palo Monte; Fon in Haitian Vodou), sacred registers of creole languages (Haitian Kreyòl ceremonial register), specialized vocabularies (Lucumí as Afro-Atlantic-adapted liturgical Yorùbá), and graphic-lexical systems (Nsibidi as both script and vocabulary system). Also includes African Christian liturgical languages: Ge'ez (Ethiopian/Eritrean Orthodox), Coptic (Egyptian Coptic Church)."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#phraseFunction> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The ceremonial function of this phrase. Range: iroko:SpeechActTypeScheme."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#pos-other> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Part of speech not covered by existing concepts."@en .
