<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#lang-graphic-lexical> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A system in which graphic signs function as lexical or semiotic units, functioning analogously to writing. Examples: Nsibidi (Ejagham) as a graphic communication system; Anaforuana signs with lexical referents. Linked to iroko-veve for the graphic dimension."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#inLanguage> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#publicDefinition> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#LexicalSense> .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#naming-nkisi-palo> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Nkisi name (Palo)"@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#speechActType> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Links a LiturgicalPhrase to the SacredSpeechAct type it instantiates — oath, invocation, greeting, prohibition, praise, or similar. SacredSpeechAct is a classification of the ceremonial function a phrase performs within ritual protocol; this property tags the phrase with that type. A phrase may instantiate more than one speech act type. Access: community-only."@en .
<https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#inLanguage> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <https://ontology.irokosociety.org/iroko#SacredLanguage> .
